<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Taifa of Albarracín</h1><p class="st-meta">1012 CE – 1104 CE (approx.) · capital Albarracín</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A mountain taifa in the Sierra de Albarracín ruled for nearly a century by the Berber Banu Razin, one of the longest-lived small states of al-Andalus.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Banu Razin, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> family of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawwara">Hawwara</a> who had held the district of al-Sahla since the 8th century, declared independence around 1012 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudhayl-ibn-khalaf-ibn-razin">Hudhayl ibn Khalaf ibn Razin</a> as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba</a> broke apart.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albarracin">Albarracín</a> was a rugged frontier lordship of castles, pasture and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>. Its survival owed much to the long reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-malik-ibn-hudhayl">Abd al-Malik ibn Hudhayl</a> (c. 1045-1103), who paid tribute to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">El Cid</a> when necessary. The town's walls and its compact old quarter date in part from this period.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albarracin">Albarracín</a> in 1104, soon after the death of Abd al-Malik. In 1170 the Navarrese noble <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-ruiz-de-azagra">Pedro Ruiz de Azagra</a> received the town, founding the semi-independent <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-albarracin">Lordship of Albarracín</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/taifa-of-albarracin.jpg" alt="Taifa of Albarracín" width="320" height="271"><figcaption>Image: Tyk · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Location_map_Taifa_of_Albarrac%C3%ADn.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1012 CE – 1104 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Albarracín</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hudhayl ibn Khalaf ibn Razin, Abd al-Malik ibn Hudhayl, Yahya ibn Abd al-Malik</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Andalusi Arabic, Berber</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Taifa of al-Sahla, Banu Razin</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1058&amp;focus=taifa-of-albarracin" aria-label="Taifa of Albarracín on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4645 734 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Taifa of Albarracín lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4965 990L4881 1071M4965 990L4799 1289M4965 990L4965 990"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4881 1071h0M4799 1289h0M4965 990h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4965 990h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4965 990h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Albarracín. <a href="/atlas?year=1058&amp;focus=taifa-of-albarracin">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
